.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/supports-color Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "supports-color [![Build Status](https://travis-ci.org/chalk/supports-color.svg?branch=master)](https://travis-ci.org/chalk/supports-color)"
.PP
.RS 4
Detect whether a terminal supports color 
.RE
.PP
.SH "Install"
.PP
.PP
.nf
$ npm install supports\-color
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
.PP
.nf
const supportsColor = require('supports\-color');

if (supportsColor\&.stdout) {
    console\&.log('Terminal stdout supports color');
}

if (supportsColor\&.stdout\&.has256) {
    console\&.log('Terminal stdout supports 256 colors');
}

if (supportsColor\&.stderr\&.has16m) {
    console\&.log('Terminal stderr supports 16 million colors (truecolor)');
}
.fi
.PP
.SH "API"
.PP
Returns an \fRObject\fP with a \fRstdout\fP and \fRstderr\fP property for testing either streams\&. Each property is an \fRObject\fP, or \fRfalse\fP if color is not supported\&.

.PP
The \fRstdout\fP/\fRstderr\fP objects specifies a level of support for color through a \fR\&.level\fP property and a corresponding flag:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\&.level = 1\fP and \fR\&.hasBasic = true\fP: Basic color support (16 colors)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\&.level = 2\fP and \fR\&.has256 = true\fP: 256 color support
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\&.level = 3\fP and \fR\&.has16m = true\fP: Truecolor support (16 million colors)
.PP
.SH "Info"
.PP
It obeys the \fR--color\fP and \fR--no-color\fP CLI flags\&.

.PP
For situations where using \fR--color\fP is not possible, use the environment variable \fRFORCE_COLOR=1\fP (level 1), \fRFORCE_COLOR=2\fP (level 2), or \fRFORCE_COLOR=3\fP (level 3) to forcefully enable color, or \fRFORCE_COLOR=0\fP to forcefully disable\&. The use of \fRFORCE_COLOR\fP overrides all other color support checks\&.

.PP
Explicit 256/Truecolor mode can be enabled using the \fR--color=256\fP and \fR--color=16m\fP flags, respectively\&.
